papers.abstract:
We study empirical convergence rates for $(p,q)$-Gromov-Wasserstein (GW) distances $D_{p,q}$ for arbitrary real exponents $p>0$ and $q\ge1$. Let $\mu$ and $
u$ be probability measures on compact metric spaces $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{Y}$, and let $\widehat{\mu}_n$ and $\widehat{
u}_m$ be their empirical measures, constructed from independent i.i.d. samples of sizes $n$ and $m$. If the spaces $\mathcal{X},\,\mathcal{Y}$ are compact subsets of the Euclidean spaces $\mathbb{R}^{d_x}$ and $\mathbb{R}^{d_y}$, respectively, we obtain an upper bound of order $(n\wedge m)^{-(p\wedge2)/((d_x\wedge d_y)\vee 2(p\wedge2))}$ on the plug-in error of $D_{p,q}^p(\widehat{\mu}_n,\widehat{
u}_m)$ in expectation, up to a logarithmic factor at the critical dimension $d_x\wedge d_y=2(p\wedge2)$. On Euclidean balls, this rate is sharp up to the logarithmic factor. More generally, we show that the same upper bound holds when one space is a compact subset of a complete, connected $d$-dimensional Riemannian manifold and the other has polynomial covering numbers, with $d_x\wedge d_y$ replaced by $d$. Earlier works on $(2,2)$-GW, $(2,1)$-GW and other even-order cases rely on variational representations of GW distances that are specific to their exponents. We instead reduce the plug-in error to an analysis of empirical processes through local duality, which may be of independent interest.

We formulate magnetostatics in $\mathbb{R}^3$ as a theory of stresses in which the vector potential $\mathbf{A}$ replaces the virtual velocity field of continuum mechanics. A current system is a collection of linear functionals on vector potentials, one for each subbody, whose values are interpreted as minus the potential energy. Cauchy's theorem gives a tensor field $\mathbf{\Sigma}$, the analog of the traction stress, such that the surface current on the boundary is $\mathbf{\Sigma} oldsymbol{
u}$. We assume only weak gauge invariance, that is, a gauge transformation $\mathbf{A}\mapsto\mathbf{A}+
abla\lambda$ changes the energy only through the values of $
abla\lambda$. This is the analog of the symmetry of the stress, and it holds if and only if $\mathbf{\Sigma}$ is skew-symmetric, so that it is represented by a vector field $\mathbf{M}$. The energy then takes the form $-\int(\mathbf{j}_f\cdot\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{M}\cdot\mathbf{B})\,dV$, which splits every current system uniquely into free current $\mathbf{j}_f$ and magnetization $\mathbf{M}$, with bound currents $
abla	imes\mathbf{M}$ and $\mathbf{M}	imes oldsymbol{
u}$. Gauge invariance of the energy is the analog of the balance of forces, and it singles out the current systems without free current. For currents embedded in matter, the force consists of the Lorentz force on the total current, an asymmetric active stress whose skew-symmetric part is the couple density $\mathbf{M}	imes\mathbf{B}$, and a gauge-dependent term that appears only where free current crosses the boundary of the subbody. This term is the rate at which the charge carriers transport potential momentum out of the subbody, so that the force on the matter itself is the gauge-invariant part.
